title Caravan revoke -- reach returns the moment its work is carried
prompt Kyri first, then REMEMBER and the road-ahead itineraries; Standfast on reds, then the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; council rota one doc; send each finished increment
think Standfast first. Walked crux/REDS.md whole -- rows 80 and 83 through 87 each read closed, with #85's reopen-and-close on 20260819.134229 the most recent, and its loom standing as a check rather than a habit. Nothing agent-closable stood open, so the line was clear for constructive work.
think Council rota by lap index: 485 .kyri logs on disk when the lap opened, so lap 486, and 486 mod 27 = 0, which wraps to item 27 -- recursion-prompts/README.md, the cellar charter. Read it whole. It names the balance compass this run is asked to optimize for, and it carried one stale citation from the work-in-progress to crux rename, which this round repairs on touch.
think The running thread was named plainly by the last log's recommend, and confer.rye had named it three ways -- module prose, README limit line, and witness header alike. A conferred word grows a child's capability list and never prunes it. Revocation is the mirror, and a real microkernel deletes a cspace slot rather than merely declining to add one.
think The first design question was what the wire carries, and the mirror answered it. Confer publishes the words themselves and then the count that makes them live; revoke publishes one number and nothing else -- how many conferred words remain live -- because the words list is already whole in the child's hands. One shrinking number is the entire message.
think The second question was how a child deletes, since capabilities.ChildSlot grants and never prunes. Chose a rebuild rather than a removal: the child keeps a copy of the slot it was spawned with and reconstructs base plus the leading kept words. Total by construction, the spawn line never at risk, and the conferred words returning in exactly the order they were granted.
think The third question was the hard half the recommend had already named -- when a revocation must refuse. Arcs are conferred in order and their work is carried in order, so the run tracks a served count beside the taken count, and revoke_refusal refuses RevokeInflight whenever any arc being pruned is one whose work still stands. The check reduces to served == conferred, which is honest and small.
think The fourth question was what number the two rooms disagree on. Named it stale reach -- arcs a child can still touch after the work that reach served is already carried -- the exact mirror of the dark reach confer closed. Holding leaves it above zero at the moment a child comes home; revoking drives it to zero with the same children carrying the same work.
think The fifth question arrived from a RED path rather than from design, and it is the best thing in the round. The first shape had the child prune and then answer, and the supervisor derived its stale-reach number from the keep it had chosen rather than from what the child reported. Planting a child that hears the revocation and keeps every word anyway ran GREEN while the child's own line read 1 still reachable at home. The run believed a deletion that never happened.
think Closed that by making the answer earned rather than intended: the child probes the arcs it just returned and publishes .pruned only once they genuinely refuse it. A rebuild that leaves any of them reachable publishes nothing, and the run refuses the whole record with RevokeUnpruned. Re-ran the same planted RED and it now refuses in the run itself rather than only in the witness's eye.
obs The rung earns four refusals rather than five, and saying so plainly is better than manufacturing a fifth. Direction, supply, work still standing, and the answer are the four genuinely distinct ways a revocation can be wrong; the room in the child's hands belongs to the conferring direction alone, since a pruning only ever frees room.
obs The whole file compiled clean on the first build and the selftest ran GREEN on the first pass, which is worth naming honestly rather than treating as luck: the arc's habit is now thick enough that a new rung is mostly the shape of the rung below with one act inverted. The interesting work was entirely in the two questions the habit could not answer -- the in-flight refusal and the earned answer.
obs The generalization test held one tier further out, all 9 pairs: a run that revokes nothing reads as unhand.admissible_held reads it. The door needed no change at all, which is the right result -- everything this rung moves stands on the child's side, exactly where the elder limit lived.
obs Docs stayed synced in the same round. Confer's README limit paragraph said revocation belonged to the vision room until a witness binds it; a witness binds it now, so that paragraph points at Revoke, and the module table gained its row in the same edit.
loom witness=caravan_revoke_witness result=GREEN phases=4 children=3 homed=1 conferred=1 revoked_held=0 revoked_pruned=1 stale_held=1 stale_pruned=0 pairs_agreed=9 red_paths_proven=5
file caravan/revoke.rye the mirror act -- one shrinking number, a slot the child rebuilds, four refusals, and an answer earned by the child's own probe
file tools/caravan_revoke_witness.rish the witness on metal, reading every claim natively and naming all five proven RED paths
file caravan/README.md the Revoke section, the module table row, and confer's limit line repointed now that a witness binds it
file crux/REMEMBER.md the live edge moved to this rung, the confer edge condensed to a pointer
file recursion-prompts/README.md one stale work-in-progress citation repointed to crux on touch -- references are promises
recommend keep-going the arc's next question is already visible from this rung's own shape: every act so far moves a line or a word between one supervisor and one child, and the supervisor is still a single point of trust. Whether a child may DELEGATE -- handing a strict subset of its own reach to a grandchild it spawns, with the door weighing the grandchild's line against the child's rather than against the document's ceiling -- is the next honest rung, and the hard half will be proving a grandchild can never hold a right its parent lacks.
